{
  "version": 1,
  "language": "spanish",
  "chapter": 266,
  "title": "Review --- Four Last Pieces, and the Thread",
  "drivablePrefix": 5,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:3d43d903fdc6b620",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C67-repaso-a1",
      "sequence": 2565,
      "title": "(review)",
      "headword": "(review)",
      "romanization": "(review)",
      "gloss": "four last pieces — and the thread that has run through the whole of this level",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:772d151d4845d417",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first five in order. (Primero, segundo, tercero, cuarto, quinto.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four chants."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "First. El primer libro, la primera casa — ordinals go in front, and two of them drop their o before a masculine noun."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Second. Uno es grande, otro es bueno — a pair split across the sentence. Otro libro, with no article in front of it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third. Comer es bueno — the infinitive naming an activity, where English would reach for -ing and Spanish will not."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Fourth. Hoy como en casa — the when and the where can move, and the front is where the emphasis lives."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the thread",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Primer is the third time you have watched Spanish bite the ending off a word leaning on another — after muy and mal. Three sightings make a habit, not a list of exceptions, and you will meet a fourth without being surprised."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And the moving pieces close a thread that has run through this whole level. Latin marked who did what with endings. Those endings fell away, and Spanish built replacements out of small audible things: an article to say which one, a preposition to mark the person on the receiving end, a comma to address somebody, and a fixed front position to carry emphasis."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Every one of those was a chapter of its own. Together they are one story, and you now have all of it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the four chants, in order",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the four chants, in order]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-a1-inf",
              "prompt": "Eating is good -- comer es bueno, or comiendo es bueno?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-INFINITIVE-NOT-GERUND"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 13,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "comer es bueno",
                "comer es bueno, the infinitive"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The gerund never names an activity.",
                "incorrect": "Comer es bueno."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-a1-apocope",
              "prompt": "How many times have you now seen a word drop its ending before another word?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-ORDINAL-APOCOPE"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "three",
                "three: muy, mal, primer"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "A habit, not a list.",
                "incorrect": "Three -- muy, mal and now primer."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-a1-otro",
              "prompt": "Does otro take an article?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-OTRO"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 11,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "no",
                "no, never"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Otro libro, not un otro libro.",
                "incorrect": "No."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Where does emphasis live in a Spanish sentence? (At the front.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And what one event explains the article, the personal a, the vocative comma and that emphatic position? (Latin's endings falling away.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C67-repaso-lo-que-reemplazo-los-casos",
      "sequence": 2566,
      "title": "Review — one name, four different jobs",
      "headword": "(review)",
      "romanization": "(review)",
      "gloss": "Latin's endings fell off and four small things took over their work — a preposition, a comma, an article, and where the word sits",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:44d8363d8a67a6c2",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "\"I see María.\" (Veo a María.) \"María, come.\" (María, ven.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The name does not change. María is María in both. Everything that distinguishes them sits outside the word."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Veo a María. — she is what the seeing lands on. María, ven. — she is the one being spoken to."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A preposition in the first, a comma in the second. Latin did both with endings on María herself, and had a whole case for each."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The article does a third job: a name points at one person already, so el has nothing left to do — María, not la María. Give her a title and it comes back: la señora García."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The fourth job went to position, which decides form as well as meaning."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mucha agua. — leaning on a noun, so it agrees. Come mucho. — leaning on a verb, so it freezes."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Same word, two shapes, and the only difference is what it stands next to. The ordinal does this one slot over: el primer libro, in front, shortened."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And because the verb's ending already says who did what, the pieces that say when and where can move:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hoy como en casa., Como en casa hoy., En casa como hoy."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three placements, one meaning — and the front slot is the emphatic one. Latin bought that freedom with case endings; Spanish lost them and bought it back with word order. Same trade in ¡Qué grande!: the force sits at the front."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "hoy como en casa, como en casa hoy, en casa como hoy",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *hoy como en casa*, *como en casa hoy*, *en casa como hoy*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-lo-que-reemplazo-los-casos-dos",
              "prompt": "I see Maria, then Maria, come.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-PERSONAL-A-WHY",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-VOCATIVE-NOT-OBJECT"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "veo a maria; maria, ven",
                "veo a maria, maria ven"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "A preposition, then a comma — the name itself never moved.",
                "incorrect": "Veo a Maria; Maria, ven."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-lo-que-reemplazo-los-casos-titulo",
              "prompt": "Talk about Mrs Garcia in the third person, with her title.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-PROPER-NOUN-NO-ARTICLE",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-TITLE-TAKES-ARTICLE"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 12,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "la senora garcia",
                "la señora garcia"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The title brings the article back that the bare name refused.",
                "incorrect": "La senora Garcia."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-lo-que-reemplazo-los-casos-cuantificador",
              "prompt": "A lot of water, then he eats a lot.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-QUANTIFIER-AGREES-WITH-NOUN",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-QUANTIFIER-FROZEN-AFTER-VERB"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "mucha agua; come mucho",
                "mucha agua, come mucho"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Next to a noun it agrees; next to a verb there is nothing to agree with.",
                "incorrect": "Mucha agua; come mucho."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-lo-que-reemplazo-los-casos-orden",
              "prompt": "Say today I eat at home, putting the emphasis on today.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-CIRCUMSTANTIAL-MOVES",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-WORD-ORDER-EMPHASIS",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-ORDINAL-BEFORE-NOUN"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 12,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "hoy como en casa",
                "hoy, como en casa"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Front slot, so hoy carries the weight.",
                "incorrect": "Hoy como en casa."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-lo-que-reemplazo-los-casos-que",
              "prompt": "Say how big!",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-EXCLAMATIVE-QUE"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "que grande",
                "¡qué grande!",
                "qué grande"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Que at the front, carrying the force, and keeping its accent.",
                "incorrect": "Que grande."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What replaced Latin's ending for the person on the receiving end? (A little a.) Which slot carries emphasis? (The front one.) And why does mucho refuse to agree in come mucho? (It leans on a verb.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C67-primera-lectura",
      "sequence": 2567,
      "title": "(reading) — the first paragraph",
      "headword": "(reading)",
      "romanization": "(reading)",
      "gloss": "the first paragraph — forty-seven words of Spanish in a row, and not one of them new",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:b499629ab3bfd79b",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Everything you are about to read, you already know. Not one word in it is new."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is new is that there are forty-seven of them in a row."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "comprehension",
          "title": "Reading",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "María vive en una casa grande con su hermano. Hoy no trabaja, porque es domingo. Por la mañana come pan. Luego va a leer un libro. El libro es muy bueno, pero es bastante grande. Ahora está cansada. Va a dormir. Mañana habla español con su estudiante."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Read it once without stopping. Do not translate as you go — let the sentences arrive."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now read it again, and notice how little work it took."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Look at what is holding it together, because it is not vocabulary."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Porque joins a fact to its reason. Pero holds two true things apart. Luego puts one thing after another. Va a reaches forward into tomorrow. Each of those was a chapter, and each was small."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Eight sentences across six lines is not much. But the longest run of Spanish anywhere in this book so far was a single line of about fifteen words, and nearly every one of those was a drill — a list of verb forms, or one phrase set against another — rather than something a person would actually say."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is the first time the book has asked you to hold a whole paragraph in your head at once — and the reason it can is that everything in it was paid for, one chapter at a time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the paragraph aloud, once, without stopping",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the paragraph aloud, once, without stopping]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-primera-lectura-who",
              "prompt": "Who does Maria live with?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-SKILL-CONNECTED-READING"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "her brother",
                "su hermano",
                "with her brother"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Con su hermano.",
                "incorrect": "Her brother -- con su hermano."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-primera-lectura-why",
              "prompt": "Why is she not working today?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-SKILL-CONNECTED-READING"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "because it is sunday",
                "porque es domingo",
                "it is sunday"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Porque es domingo.",
                "incorrect": "Because it is Sunday."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-primera-lectura-book",
              "prompt": "Two things are said about the book. What are they?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-SKILL-CONNECTED-READING"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 16,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "it is good but big",
                "muy bueno pero bastante grande",
                "good and quite large"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Muy bueno, pero bastante grande.",
                "incorrect": "It is very good, but quite big."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "How many words in that paragraph were new to you? (None.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And what made it readable? (Everything in it had already been paid for, one chapter at a time.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C67-segunda-lectura",
      "sequence": 2568,
      "title": "(reading) — the second paragraph",
      "headword": "(reading)",
      "romanization": "(reading)",
      "gloss": "the second paragraph — a different person, a different day, and still nothing new",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:217d918dc1d41230",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The last paragraph was about María. This one is about her brother, on a different day."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Same rule as before: not one word in it is new."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "comprehension",
          "title": "Reading",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "El lunes María trabaja. Su hermano no. Está en casa con el perro. Por la tarde bebe café y escribe a su madre. No hay pan, pero hay agua. Luego va a salir, porque quiere ver a su padre. Por la noche los dos comen en casa."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Once through, without stopping."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Again — and this time notice that you did not have to work out who Está refers to. Spanish left the name out and you kept it anyway."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Su hermano no. Two words, and the verb is gone. Spanish drops what the sentence before it already said, and the reader carries it forward."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is the thing a paragraph can do that a sentence cannot. A sentence has to say everything. A paragraph gets to lean on the one before it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is also why reading gets easier as it gets longer, which is the opposite of what most people expect."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
